45799451-d7b1-5abf-840c-3d93dcdaeb60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338860 #edb/ref 1074 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $T$ is $p=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$  and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}],\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338863 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3cd142bb-0e37-5169-9871-c05d6282caa6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338863 #edb/ref 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨3,0,4⟩$ and $w=⟨1,-1,1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+j-3k \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨4,1,-3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338864 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(1,\\sqrt{2},-1)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨0,11,\\frac{1}{3}⟩+s⟨1,2,-3⟩$ and $r=⟨0,\\sqrt{2},5⟩+t⟨-1,0,3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1d4f201-ef5e-5eaf-9add-578e8923ebb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338864 #edb/ref 1074 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨1,2,-3⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨-1,0,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(1,\\sqrt{2},-1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $c=⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩+s⟨1,2,-3⟩+t⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338866 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338866 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338866 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338866 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $E(16,-1,3)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨9,-7,2⟩+s⟨5,1,-2⟩$ and $r=⟨15,2,-3⟩+t⟨1,11,3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338866 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc807ce5-aee1-5714-82d8-8391abd01453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338866 #edb/ref 1074 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨5,1,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,11,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $E(16,-1,3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $e=⟨16,-1,3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =e+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨16,-1,3⟩+s⟨5,1,-2⟩+t⟨1,11,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387887 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387887 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387887 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387887 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $A(7,-2,3)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨9,2,1⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩$ and $r=⟨4,-5,19⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387887 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d8e167e-ba7b-5746-8896-10f0f88087c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387887 #edb/ref 1074 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨2,-2,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨3,4,7⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $A(7,-2,3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $a=⟨7,-2,3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387888 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387888 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387888 #edb/ref 1078